Burntime - the player's manual
==============================

This describes the game as this program plays it: the remaster 1.3's rules,
in its Classic and Extended presets. The numbers are the program's own.
Where the two presets differ, both are given.


1. Starting a game
------------------

The start screen asks who plays. Click a name field to take part, click the
face to change it (right click goes back); START needs at least one face.
Two people can play, one after the other, each with a turn of their own.

The three toggles choose:

- the difficulty;
- the rules: Classic (the 1993 game's numbers) or Extended (the remaster's
  additions: armour, a shared supply pool, random traders);
- the machine opponents: Modern (the remaster's own), Amiga (the Amiga
  version's opponent) or None (empty seats).

The first time, the screen opens on Extended with the Modern opponent, as
the remaster's does; after that, like the remaster 1.3, it opens with the
difficulty, rules and opponent of the last game you started. Each player's
flag chooses his colour (green, red, blue or gray); his people wear it.

LOAD opens the saved games.


2. Turns and days
-----------------

Each player has a turn of 240 seconds, shown by the bar at the top. The turn
ends when the time runs out or when you set out on a journey. A day ends
when no player is left to act; then everybody eats and drinks, camps
produce, and the wounded heal.

On the world map, click a neighbouring place to travel there. A road takes
the days the map gives it; the days pass by themselves. Click the place you
stand at to go in. Before every journey the game saves itself
(autosave-1 and autosave-2, the older of the two is replaced).


3. Daily needs
--------------

Everybody eats 1 food and drinks 1 water a day. Each need that cannot be met
costs 25 health that day. A group refills from what it carries: in Classic
each person from their own pack (water from the camp's reserve or the
smallest container, food from the smallest item); in Extended the group
shares one supply pool.

Health comes back by itself only at 70 or more: +2 a day. A doctor in the
group or in the camp heals +4 a day at 50 or more. In Extended a doctor also gives +1 a
day below 50 to anybody who has eaten and drunk. Nobody goes above 100.


4. Water
--------

Fill empty containers at a spring or a well; a larger container carries
more. A camp's spring yields water every day.

A pump makes a camp's spring give more. It works only when it lies in the
room that holds the water source, and pumps do not add up: an industrial
pump counts if there is one, otherwise a hand pump.

- Classic: a hand pump gives the spring's yield plus a quarter (at least 3),
  an industrial pump plus a half (at least 6).
- Extended: a hand pump adds 2, an industrial pump 5.


5. Food and camps
-----------------

Leave a follower in a place to make it your camp. The camp's people are fed
first from what it makes; what is left is stored, up to 6 items.

Traps and tools placed in the camp's rooms make food each day. Only what
lies in the rooms counts, not what people carry.

    Tool        counted   food a day with 1 / 2
    rat trap    up to 2   3 / 4
    snake trap  up to 2   4 / 5
    trap        up to 2   5 / 7
    knife       1         2 (3 with two or more people)

The camp picks its best production by itself, as soon as it is founded,
unless you choose one.

A camp is defended by the people you leave in it: each one has to be beaten
before the place is taken. A city cannot be your camp: there your people
cannot be left behind or sent away.


6. Dangers
----------

A place's INFO shows gas and radiation. Gas costs 0.5 health a second,
radiation 1.35, less by the best protection in the person's own pack (a
gas mask, a protective suit). Anyone who stays in or camps at such a place
without full protection dies at the end of the day. One face is immune to
gas; none is immune to radiation.


7. People
---------

Talk to people to hire them. They ask for a snake, meat or a water bottle,
or a particular item; the first of these found in your pack is paid. They
join only if your leader is experienced enough (the leader's experience
times 1.5 must reach theirs). A group holds five, the leader included.

What a new recruit brings: in Classic 9 food and 5 water; in Extended what
you paid them with if it is food, and some water by difficulty.

Technicians build. Right click an item with a technician selected and, if
it is the main part of something, he tells you what is needed. Rat traps,
traps, pumps, the two-way radio and the protective suit only a technician
can build; loading a rifle or a pistol anybody can. What he has told you
once, the item's description remembers.

Doctors heal the group they travel with or the camp they stay in (see 3).
A city's doctor heals for medicine.

A two-way radio reports what happens in the places next to where you stand
and next to your camps that have a radio stored. It works while you carry
one or one is stored in the camp where you stand; INFO there then also
shows your people in that camp.


8. Fighting
-----------

Experience has four tiers, one every 25 points, and a weapon's damage rises
with the tier. Mercenaries (and traders) fight with their full experience,
everybody else with half.

Reach decides who strikes first: a defender whose weapon reaches a class
further hits an approaching attacker before he arrives.

Firearms do heavy damage and use one round a shot. When a gun is empty it
reloads from ammunition in the pack, if there is any. Your people use a
firearm only when you have selected it.

Armour (a leather jacket, a helmet) counts only in Extended: it takes its percentage
off each blow, never below 1.


9. Trading
----------

Traders sit in the cities. A trade goes through when what you offer is worth at
least what you ask. Your offer counts in full at the easiest difficulty, at
90% and 80% at the harder ones. In Classic each trader keeps his own stock and restocks as
in 1993; in Extended stocks are random, from the remaster 1.3's lists and
prices. Cities are also where you recruit,
eat, drink and see a doctor.


10. Winning and losing
----------------------

You win by holding every place that lies on a road next to a city. Holding
a place means having one of your people camped there. Only a living player
wins; a player left alone when the last rival dies is offered the victory.

You lose when your own character dies; your camps and followers are lost
with him.


11. Saving
----------

OPTIONS, then the Saves page: [NEW SAVE] writes a new one, a saved game is
loaded or overwritten from the list. The autosaves are listed in brackets.
On AROS the saves are kept in the Saves drawer of your data drawer (see
ReadMe), so updating or removing the program leaves them.


12. Controls
------------

The mouse plays as in the original: left click to walk, enter, take and
choose; right click for the menu.

The keyboard and a game controller play as the remaster 1.3 does:

    arrows              walk the selected character, or move the selection
    W A S D             pan the view
    Tab, Shift+Tab      pick the next or previous target
    Space               act on the target (talk, take, enter)
    Q                   attack, or travel on the map
    Shift+Left/Right    change the selected character
    Escape              open the menu, or close what is open
    T (held)            end the turn
    O  H  I  R  C       options, statistics, inventory, information,
                        fight or speak
    V or M              the world map
    F8                  the remaster's graphics or the original's
    F11, Alt+Enter      window or full screen

The full list, and the controller's buttons, are in settings.txt's
[keyboard] and [gamepad] sections. This release is made for the mouse and
the keyboard: the controller support has not been tried with a real
controller.


13. Settings and music
----------------------

OPTIONS, then Settings: the graphics (the remaster's or the original's,
also F8), full screen (also F11), the input prompts and their glyphs, the
language, the music (the remaster's DOS set, the Amiga set, or off) and the
map music:

- None: the maps are quiet;
- Keep: the song of the scene you left carries on over the map, ends, and
  the playlist follows;
- List: the maps play a playlist in random order, paused while you are
  away and resumed when you come back.

Your choices are kept between games (ENV:Burntime/user.txt on AROS).
